{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval_new/seed1572225882/4/codegen_prompts/2.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1572225882/4`\n- 题号：`2`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/eval_new/seed1572225882/4/2.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"2\">\n- `description`：将网格中散落的彩色碎片沿箭头指向的方向滑动，按碎片的面积从小到大依次堆叠到箭头尖端所在的目标框架内。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为黑色（black）。\n  - 网格中有一个用 maroon 颜色绘制的矩形框架（空心），框架内部为黑色。\n  - 框架外散布着若干彩色碎片（每个碎片是一个由单一非背景色构成的连通区域，形状不规则但面积各不相同）。\n  - 每个碎片旁边有一个由同色像素组成的箭头标记：箭头由一条长度为 2 的直线段加上末端一个垂直于方向的 1 像素构成（形如 \">\"），箭头指向上、下、左、右四个方向之一，且箭头方向总是指向框架的方向。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步：计算每个碎片的面积（像素数量），按面积从小到大排序。\n  - 第二步：按排序顺序依次处理每个碎片。将碎片沿其箭头指向的方向平移，直到碎片的前沿接触到框架内壁或已放置的碎片为止。\n  - 第三步：碎片进入框架后保持原始形状不变，箭头标记在输出中消失。\n  - 框架本身保持不变。碎片之间不重叠——后放入的碎片堆叠在先放入的碎片之上（即靠近框架入口侧）。\n\n- `Key insight`：核心难点在于发现碎片的放置顺序由面积决定——面积最小的碎片最先到达框架最深处，面积最大的碎片最后放置、离入口最近。仅看到箭头方向容易误以为碎片是同时滑入的，但堆叠结果表明存在一个隐含的排序规则。解题者需要从多个样例中归纳出\"面积排序\"这一非显然的前置逻辑。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/89565ca0, eval/581f7754, train/465b7d93, train/ba1aa698, eval/8b7bacbf, train/17829a00, eval/58490d8a, eval/88e364bc\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4089\nmechanism=0.5470\nlexical=0.0134\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/89565ca0.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"colored partitioned frame line drawings; ignore scattered pad-color pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"stack one horizontal bar per frame, ordered by count of enclosed background regions then color id\",\n  \"transform\": \"bar length = number of enclosed 4-connected background regions; pad right with the scattered color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height = number of frame colors, width = maximum enclosed-region count\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4004\nmechanism=0.4677\nlexical=0.0119\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:frame, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/581f7754.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"multi-color sprite s with a unique marker color (minority color inside s)\",\n  \"dst\": \"align to isolated border pixel d (same marker color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.marker.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate s: if d on top/bottom edge → align col(s.marker) to col(d); else → align row(s.marker) to row(d); clip out-of-bounds pixels\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3876\nmechanism=0.4910\nlexical=0.0114\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:between, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/465b7d93.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/465b7d93.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"small colored sprite s outside the frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"hollow rectangular frame interior d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"unique s maps to unique d; s.bbox extremes align to d.bbox extremes\",\n  \"transform\": \"clear s; stretch s into d by mapping bbox extremes, drawing 4-adjacent segments, and filling enclosed regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3750\nmechanism=0.4892\nlexical=0.0115\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:frame, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:nearest, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ba1aa698.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ba1aa698.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.\n- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.\n- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.\n\nRule\n- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.\n- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.\n- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.\n\nKey insight\n- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"identical interior sprite instances inside the shared-border framed panels\",\n  \"path\": \"constant diagonal motion from panel to panel, continued one more standalone panel to the right; vertical offset is scaled from shared-border spacing to full panel width and rounded to the nearest row\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"stamp one copy of the sprite in the next framed panel at the extrapolated row and the same local columns\",\n  \"transform\": \"discard the previous panels; draw only the new single frame with the same frame and fill colors\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same height, width of one standalone panel\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3590\nmechanism=0.4069\nlexical=0.0114\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:marker, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8b7bacbf.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8b7bacbf.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Find single pixels on grid edges that are endpoints of lines, trace along the line to find connected closed regions, and fill those regions with the edge pixel's color.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"single colored pixels s on grid edge that are endpoints of a line\",\n  \"dst\": \"closed regions d connected to the line\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d is reachable by following the line from s\",\n  \"path\": \"trace along the continuous line from edge pixel s\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"fill interior of each closed region d encountered along path with s.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"d.interior.color = s.color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: identify single pixels on grid edge; Step 2: trace the line connected to each edge pixel; Step 3: for each closed region touching the line, flood-fill its interior with edge pixel color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"path-tracing\",\n    \"edge-detection\",\n    \"region-filling\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
